<p>I'm not saying that <em>having</em> the protocols is overly dramatic; I'm saying that the particular way these were <em>included</em> didn't feel clinical enough. That's why I referred to the other XK types. (Speaking of which, XK needn't be religious — it just means the world ends. A lot. Probably with a KABLAM, heh.)</p>
<p>I agree completely that not knowing makes it creepier. Strategic omission is the soul of fright.</p>
<p>…Though, that said, orbital dynamics are actually staggeringly predictable given sufficient data. You don't really even have to know the mechanics involved: if I remember right, the Greeks were awfully good at predicting eclipses, and they certainly didn't have Kepler's laws. If these two objects are going to collide anytime in the next several hundred years, the Foundation can probably extrapolate it out. So here's a what-if for you: What if the higher-up <em>already know</em> when these two will collide? Why are they keeping the rest of us in the dark, and what's the real oh-shit plan? With judicious [DATA EXPUNGED]s, that could be played for some serious creepiness.</p>

							</div>

