			<div class="content" id="post-content-719437">
									

<p>that definitely makes sense from a "let's make it so the readers understand" point of view. it just doesnt really for reports within the Foundation where only people who already know what it means are likely to see the reports. but having more than one end of the world classification is not going to be confusing to the reader. i'm ok with keeping "end of the world" in the terminology so long as that's not the only kind.</p>
<p>really i'm pushing for the concept of more classifications because it makes having a classification system make more sense. you shouldnt call a product something like "DXF-5500" when it's the only product your company's ever made for example.</p>
<p>the "K" is consistent of course("Keter"?) so really it's just the first letter being classified, and with "ZK" meaning "end of reality" and earlier letters being lesser things, it looks like progressing through the alphabet means more and more severe results as you go. but we've got from M to Y to play with, and really i'd not see a need for more than 4 or 5 different "end of the world" scenario types. religious related scenarios are just the type that i see used most frequently with XK. (<a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-995">http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-995</a> <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/dr-clef-s-proposal">http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/dr-clef-s-proposal</a> <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-861">http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-861</a> <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-231">http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-231</a>)</p>
<p>i'd been hoping to see that kind of thing throughout the SCP articles long before i thought of trying to write one. but it's just an idea in the end, if it doesnt happen it's not gonna be a big deal</p>

							</div>

								
					
