					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gcewing" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1258768); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1258768&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609041004" alt="gcewing" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1258768)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gcewing" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1258768); return false;" >gcewing</a></span> <span class="odate time_1324029441 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Dec 2011 09:57</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1329382">
									

<p>Megatons is fine. A megaton of TNT is a recognised unit of energy, equal to about 4.2MJ. No need to say &quot;nuclear&quot; though, just &quot;explosion&quot;.</p>
<p>I'm guessing the Foundation's reasoning is that the worst case scenario would be the total conversion of the mass of both objects to energy, although according to the calculation I just did, assuming the balls are made of solid iron or something of equal density, that would actually yield about 100 gigatons.</p>
<p>That sounds like a heck of a lot, but it's unlikely that this would result in anything even approaching an end-of-world scenario. According to Wikipedia, the 2004 Indian Ocean earthquake released about 9000 gigatons, and the earth is still in one piece.</p>
<p>To do some serious damage, you would need a lot more than that. The asteroid impact that killed the dinosaurs is estimated at about 96 teratons (96,000 gigatons). And that mostly just messed up the biosphere; the only damage to the earth itself was a 180km diameter crater.</p>
<p>To do something that would make &quot;planetary evacuation insufficient&quot; would require thinking <em>much</em> bigger.</p>

